The product page was blocking on `getInventory()` before rendering anything, including the title. That fetch uses `connection()` and runs at request time, so the whole page waited on it during client navigation.

**Fix:** Keep the static shell (the `<h1>`) in the page component and move inventory into an async child wrapped in `<Suspense>`. With `cacheComponents` enabled, the title is part of the instant static shell and shows right away on navigation; stock count and price stream in after `getInventory()` resolves.

```1:28:app/product/page.tsx
import { Suspense } from 'react'
import { getInventory } from '@/lib/data'

export const unstable_instant = true

export default function ProductPage() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Premium Widget</h1>
      <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading inventory...</p>}>
        <InventoryDetails />
      </Suspense>
    </main>
  )
}

async function InventoryDetails() {
  const inventory = await getInventory()
  // ...
}
```

`unstable_instant` helps catch regressions in dev if dynamic work ends up outside a local Suspense boundary again. The build shows `/product` as partial prerender (static HTML + streamed dynamic content).

